1: # A Configurable Reservoir Decision System for HydroCNHS: Rule-Based and Language-Model Interfaces
2: 
3: **Initial English draft for a JWRPM Technical Note — software verification scope.**
4: Author names, affiliations, acknowledgments and submission formatting remain to be confirmed.
5: 
6: ## Abstract
7: 
8: HydroCNHS couples daily hydrological simulation with human decisions through four
9: application programming interfaces. Reservoir decisions supplied by language
10: models require an additional distinction between a proposed release and the
11: release that can be physically implemented. This technical note presents an
12: extension of the HydroCNHS Dam interface that separates configurable decision
13: backends, reservoir physics, river routing and versioned audits. Three methods
14: share the same observation and action contracts: a Hanasaki-based flood-control
15: benchmark, zero-shot prompting, and prompting with a Decision Knowledge Package
16: (DKP). Francis E. Walter Reservoir provides the single-reservoir example, with
17: preserved simulated inflow and original-compatible prompts. Offline verification
18: included 162 numerical comparisons of the benchmark function, three-day examples
19: of each method, and a 31-day network with two controlled reservoirs and a passive
20: outlet. The network's maximum absolute daily system water-balance residual was
21: 3.73 × 10⁻⁹ m³, and stored routing outputs matched their independent reconstruction.
22: The language-model examples used synthetic replies and therefore establish
23: interface behavior rather than operating-policy performance. The extension
24: provides inspectable configuration, decision-to-physics records and tutorial
25: notebooks while retaining the native HydroCNHS core and calibration tools.
26: 
27: **Keywords:** HydroCNHS; reservoir operations; language models; water balance; auditability.
28: 
29: ## Introduction
30: 
31: Reservoir operation introduces human decisions into the hydrological cycle.
32: HydroCNHS already represents such decisions through Dam, RiverDiv, Conveying and
33: InSitu interfaces that place human actions within the model's routing sequence
34: (Lin et al. 2022). The present work builds on that architecture. It retains the
35: hydrological core and adds a configurable reservoir decision system at the Dam
36: interface, rather than introducing a second hydrological model.
37: 
38: A language model can propose a release from a description of inflow, storage and
39: operating knowledge. That proposal must be distinguished from a physical state
40: update: a requested release may exceed available water or outlet capacity.
41: Separating these responsibilities also makes a comparison with deterministic
42: rules interpretable. Each method can receive the same inflow and initial state,
43: while the same physical and routing code converts its requested release into
44: implemented outflow. The objective of this note is to document and verify that
45: interface, its configuration and its evidence trail. It does not test whether a
46: language model operates a reservoir better than a rule-based benchmark.
47: 
48: ## HydroCNHS and the extension
49: 
50: The bundled HydroCNHS source retains GWLF and ABCD rainfall–runoff models, Hamon
51: potential evapotranspiration, Lohmann routing, the four coupling interfaces,
52: model-building utilities, genetic-algorithm calibration, data collection,
53: performance indicators and sediment-related tools. These are inherited
54: capabilities, not contributions of this extension. A source manifest pins the
55: upstream revision and individual file hashes. Availability of the full source
56: does not imply new empirical validation of every module.
57: 
58: The added system has four responsibilities (Fig. 1). A reservoir-local decision
59: backend receives an observation and returns requested release. A common physical
60: solver applies water availability and outlet constraints, calculates spill and
61: updates storage. HydroCNHS routes the implemented total outflow through the river
62: network. Audit and trace code bind the observation, proposal, physical transition
63: and diagnostic findings to the same reservoir and day. YAML assigns one decision
64: unit to each controlled reservoir; passive outlets use their rating relationships
65: without a language-model agent. Agent history and audit state remain separate
66: between reservoirs even when they share a model profile.
67: 
68: The daily observation precedes the decision and contains the date, timestep,
69: routed inflow, starting storage and available physical context. The solver then
70: computes the end-of-day balance,
71: 
72: \[
73: S_{t+1}=S_t+Q_{in,t}\Delta t-R_{impl,t}\Delta t-V_{spill,t}-V_{evap,t}-V_{seep,t},
74: \]
75: 
76: where storage and loss volumes are in m³, flows are in m³/s, and Δt is 86,400 s.
77: Implemented controlled release and spill are injected once into downstream
78: routing. The next day's history retains the proposal and the actual total
79: outflow. Reservoir decisions therefore change storage and downstream inflow in
80: connected networks. They do not alter rainfall–runoff equations or calibrated
81: hydrological parameters. With fixed upstream inflow and one reservoir, this
82: feedback concerns reservoir operations; it does not constitute feedback to
83: catchment runoff generation.
84: 
85: The operations runner consumes supplied daily local inflows. A researcher who
86: starts with meteorological forcing first uses the native HydroCNHS model setup
87: and then connects its routed reservoir inflow through the same Dam interface.
88: Already routed reservoir inflow must not be introduced again as additional local
89: runoff. A serial network adds upstream implemented outflow, local inflow and
90: travel delay at each downstream reservoir. Tributary networks instead combine
91: the routed contributions at their confluence.
92: 
93: ## Three decision methods
94: 
95: ### Hanasaki-based daily benchmark
96: 
97: The benchmark is a daily adaptation of the non-irrigation, flood-control branch
98: associated with Hanasaki et al. (2006). The formulation is also documented by
99: Abeshu et al. (2023, Eq. 4). It serves as a reproducible decision baseline, not as
100: an official Francis E. Walter operating policy or a reservoir controller built
101: into the original HydroCNHS release.
102: 
103: Let C be reservoir capacity, q̄ the historical mean inflow, and
104: γ = C/(q̄ × 86,400 × 365.25) the ratio of capacity to annual inflow volume.
105: At initialization and at the start of an operating year, define
106: k = S_start/(αC), with α = 0.85. The daily provisional release is
107: 
108: \[
109: R^*_t=\begin{cases}
110: k\bar q,&\gamma\geq0.5,\\
111: (\gamma/0.5)^2k\bar q+[1-(\gamma/0.5)^2]Q_{in,t},&0\leq\gamma<0.5.
112: \end{cases}
113: \]
114: 
115: Requested release is max(R*ₜ, 0.1q̄). The 10% floor belongs to this benchmark;
116: it is not transferred to the language-model policies as a site-specific legal
117: requirement. The common solver still determines feasible release. Monthly
118: historical means select the first wet-to-dry transition as the operating-year
119: start, with an October fallback when no such transition exists, matching the
120: pinned reference implementation. For FE Walter the selected month is May.
121: 
122: The FE statistics use 7,305 days from 1996–2015 in the frozen simulated-inflow
123: record. Mean inflow is 18.25446484681121 m³/s using the reference conversion
124: factor. This period precedes the 2016 demonstration and is a benchmark climatology,
125: not a new calibration/validation split. Only the release decision was integrated;
126: the reference program's separate simulation driver and physical model were not
127: copied into the operations loop.
128: 
129: ### Zero-shot and DKP
130: 
131: Zero-shot supplies the original FE Walter public facts, seasonal target, daily
132: state and response contract without DKP retrieval. The DKP method adds selected
133: sections of a Decision Knowledge Package, a document that records operating
134: knowledge and its sources. The retained native builder chooses sections
135: conditionally rather than inserting the entire document. Both methods preserve
136: the reference system text, numerical formatting, state builder and response
137: schema; the DKP method also preserves retrieval and the original policy audit.
138: The system prompts are external files, and the daily variables are separately
139: exposed in trace. Exact FE compatibility is distinct from an intentionally
140: modified prompt, which requires an explicit custom setting and a new source hash.
141: 
142: The reference profiles and auditors contain reservoir-specific facts. A new
143: reservoir must supply its own profile, level–storage relationship, operating
144: knowledge and versioned checks. The native FE DKP path rejects other reservoir
145: profiles because its retrieval and auditor contain FE-specific rules. The generic
146: configured backend accepts independent prompt/knowledge packages and auditors
147: for other cases. Separate live templates retain temperature 0.7, explicit model
148: identifiers, output limits, timeout and request caps. No provider was invoked for
149: the demonstrations reported here.
150: 
151: ## FE Walter demonstration and verification
152: 
153: Francis E. Walter Reservoir was selected because the project already had frozen
154: semidistributed hydrology, operating documents and a reference decision program.
155: The study map uses the earlier Demo's formal GIS layers (Fig. 2). Seven geographic
156: model units comprise two donor, three spatial-transfer diagnostic and two residual
157: units; a dummy aggregation node in model YAML has no separate mapped footprint.
158: The retained hydrological calibration and validation periods are 1990–2010 and
159: 2011–2020. Those parameters and simulated inflows were not recalibrated or rerun
160: for this software demonstration.
161: 
162: The three single-reservoir examples use the same physical case and frozen inflow
163: for January 1–3, 2016. The reference initial storage is 1,992.61 acre-ft, retained
164: as an assumption rather than relabeled as an observed starting condition. The
165: network example covers January 2016 with FE Walter and Beltzville as separate
166: controlled reservoirs and Prompton as a passive outlet. FE Walter and Beltzville
167: contribute through the Lehigh system; Prompton contributes through the Lackawaxen
168: system. The reported managed outlet sums these reservoir contributions, not the
169: entire Delaware River discharge. Identity unit hydrographs test connectivity and
170: water accounting; separate regression tests exercise delayed routing, channel
171: storage, serial reservoirs and confluence.
172: 
173: Verification addressed numerical compatibility, physical conservation and
174: evidence consistency. The benchmark release function matched the pinned reference
175: function in all 162 differential cases, including both capacity-ratio branches
176: and the environmental floor. Backend tests also covered initialization, operating-
177: year changes, chronology and invalid statistics. Prompt/profile tests covered
178: original FE rendering and cross-reservoir isolation. Independent saved-output
179: verification reconstructed storage transitions and routed volumes, checked
180: decision/audit bindings and required zero model calls for offline acceptance.
181: 
182: | Example | Duration | Maximum absolute reservoir residual (m³) | Maximum absolute system residual (m³) | Routing reconstruction difference (m³/s) | Model calls |
183: |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
184: | Rule benchmark | 3 days | 2.33 × 10⁻¹⁰ | 2.33 × 10⁻¹⁰ | 0 | 0 |
185: | Zero-shot, synthetic replies | 3 days | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
186: | DKP, synthetic replies | 3 days | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
187: | Two controlled reservoirs + passive Prompton | 31 days / 93 reservoir-days | 3.03 × 10⁻⁹ | 3.73 × 10⁻⁹ | 0 | 0 |
188: 
189: **Table 1.** Absolute daily residuals from independent verification of stored
190: offline runs. Routing difference compares reconstructed and stored outputs.
191: The language-model rows contain synthetic replies and are not performance trials.
192: 
193: All four runs passed file, physical and routing verification. Each single-
194: reservoir run retained three configured audit PASS records. The network retained
195: 62 PASS records for controlled decisions and 31 UNKNOWN records for passive
196: Prompton, whose operating strategy was not evaluated by those decision auditors.
197: Operating-strategy acceptance remained unset. These results establish that
198: decisions can pass through a common physical/routing implementation with a
199: consistent trace. They do not rank the three policies; equal fixture trajectories
200: do not establish equal language-model behavior.
201: 
202: ## Auditability, reuse and limits
203: 
204: Audits record the selected rubric, implementation version, source hashes and
205: decision binding. PASS means the configured checks passed; FAIL, WARN and UNKNOWN
206: retain their diagnostic meanings. Audit does not silently replace a requested
207: action, while physical limits are applied by the solver. Invalid, incomplete or
208: transport-failed model replies stop before release is applied. Compact daily logs
209: link to full prompt, retrieval, schema and audit evidence in raw records.
210: An independent verifier checks continuity and conservation from stored files;
211: hashes establish byte consistency, not empirical policy validity.
212: 
213: Fixed upstream forcing limits the present demonstrations to operation and routing
214: feedback. Identity routing does not establish downstream travel-time accuracy.
215: The assumed initial storage, simplified seasonal target, differing physical and
216: prompt capacities, DKP schedule applicability and environmental-flow exceptions
217: remain explicit conditions for a real FE Walter experiment. Each additional
218: reservoir requires its own sourced physical and policy settings. The existing
219: five-year diagnostic trajectory, including policy audit failures and failed or
220: unknown requests, is preserved and is not promoted to an accepted operating
221: strategy by the current software checks.
222: 
223: ## Conclusions
224: 
225: The extension places rule-based and language-model reservoir decisions within
226: the existing HydroCNHS Dam interface while retaining separate responsibilities
227: for physics, routing and audit. Numerical benchmark comparisons and offline
228: single- and multi-reservoir tests support the implemented interface and water
229: accounting. Researchers can use the YAML examples and executed notebooks to
230: inspect inputs, prompts, knowledge retrieval and resulting transitions before
231: configuring their own reservoirs. Assessing policy performance requires a
232: separate, explicitly designed real-model experiment with verified initial
233: conditions, routing, operating rules and stochastic replication.
234: 
235: ## Data and software availability
236: 
237: Code, source manifests, three-method settings, prompts, frozen input references,
238: editable figures, notebook sources and offline acceptance records are maintained
239: in [LLM_reservoir_agent](https://github.com/WenyuChiou/LLM_reservoir_agent).
240: The repository is currently private; public release and a permanent archival DOI
241: remain author decisions before submission. Native HydroCNHS source and its current
242: license attribution are retained. Full settings and prompt text are provided in
243: the [tutorial](../tutorials/README.md) and [settings guide](../docs/USER_GUIDE.md),
244: with the [offline evidence](../results/technical_note_offline/README.md).
